## What this vignette does

When a protein evolves, different residues drift in structure at different rates: some
positions barely move across a family of homologues, others swing widely. `msamodel`
predicts that **per-residue divergence profile** from a single structure and a model of
how mutations perturb it, governed by two selection-strength parameters, `a1`
(stability) and `a2` (activity). If you also have a *measured* divergence profile — how
much each residue actually moved across real homologues — you can **fit** those two
parameters to your data and ask how well the model explains it.

This vignette teaches that fitting workflow end to end, and — because a point estimate
without an uncertainty is not worth much — it puts a **confidence band** on every
prediction. Each prediction comes with a standard error, and the band is the mean plus or
minus a couple of standard errors. That standard error combines two sources of
uncertainty: the fit does not know `(a1, a2)` exactly, only to within a standard error,
and a small wobble in `(a1, a2)` produces a small wobble in every predicted value; and
the mutation scan the prediction is built on is itself finite (a limited number of
mutations per site), which adds its own sampling error. The reported `_se` already
combines both, so the band reflects total prediction uncertainty, not just one source.
The fit is deterministic (a numerical optimisation, no chain or burn-in), so the same
data always gives the same answer.

The workflow has four steps, drawn from three functions:

1. **fit** `(a1, a2)` to your data — `fit_lrmsd_msa_site()`. The fit also reports how
   well it does: goodness of fit comes back attached, as `fit_site$gof`, so scoring is not a
   separate call;
2. **predict** the divergence profile with a band — `predict_profiles()`;
3. band the four **nested-model variants** (the profile under each what-if selection
   scenario), and
4. band the **decomposition** (splitting one profile into its mutation, stability, and
   activity parts) — both from one function, `predict_decomposition()`.

`predict_profiles()` and `predict_decomposition()` each return **both response axes at
once** — a `$site` tibble (one value per residue) and a `$mode` tibble (one value per
normal mode of the structure, a coarser motion-based coordinate). **Part A** below reads
`$site` through the whole workflow; **Part B** reads `$mode` from the same calls. (The
[site analysis](site-analysis.html) and [mode analysis](mode-analysis.html)
vignettes develop the model itself; here the model is fixed and the subject is fitting
and uncertainty.)

## One idea to grasp first: `lrmsd` vs. `nlrmsd`

Each prediction verb takes a `metric` argument selecting one of two forms, and knowing
which to reach for is the one thing worth understanding before you start.

- **`lrmsd`** (`metric = "lrmsd"`) is the profile the model predicts directly: the log of
  each residue's structural divergence. Think of it as the raw predicted curve.
- **`nlrmsd`** (`metric = "nlrmsd"`) is that same curve with its average subtracted off:
  `nlrmsd = lrmsd - mean(lrmsd)`. It describes the *shape* of the profile — which
  residues are above or below average — with the overall level removed. The leading
  `n` marks this centred form.

Why keep both? Because they answer different questions, and each carries its **own**
confidence band. It is tempting to assume `nlrmsd`'s band is just `lrmsd`'s band shifted
down by the mean — but it is genuinely narrower. The reason: the mean you subtract is
itself computed from the model, so it moves with `(a1, a2)` too. When `(a1, a2)` wobble,
the whole curve and its mean wobble *together*, and part of the wobble cancels in
`lrmsd - mean(lrmsd)`. The shape is thus more tightly pinned down than the raw curve, and
its band is correspondingly tighter. So these are two real predictions with two real
uncertainties, not one prediction drawn twice.

**Which to use:** compare against measured data with **`nlrmsd`**. Real divergence
profiles can only be compared up to an overall level (the data does not pin down the
absolute scale), so the meaningful comparison is of shape — exactly what `nlrmsd` holds.
Reach for **`lrmsd`** when you want the model's raw predicted curve on its own. The
decomposition (step 4) exists only in the centred form, because there is no meaningful
way to split up an overall level the data cannot see.

## From a standard error to a band

Every prediction verb reports each value with a `_se` sibling column — the standard
error of that value. To draw a confidence band, turn the standard error into
lower/upper bounds: a 95% band is the value plus or minus `z_975` standard errors, with
`z_975 = qnorm(0.975) ≈ 1.96`. We define that multiplier once and reuse it in every plot
below (for a 99% band use `qnorm(0.995) ≈ 2.58` instead):

```{r zconst}
z_975 <- qnorm(0.975)   # 1.96; the multiplier for a 95% band
```

# Part A — Site axis (read `$site`)

## A1. Setup: from your structure to a fittable object

The workflow needs two things from you: a **structure** (a PDB file) and its
**active-site residues** (a vector of residue numbers). Everything else the package
builds. We use the worked example `1znb_A` (zinc beta-lactamase) that ships with the
package; replace the two marked lines with your own protein.

First, attach the packages. `msamodel` is the package itself; the other three are used
by the data-wrangling and plotting code shown throughout (ggplot2 is optional — skip it
if you only want the numeric tables).

```{r attach}
library(msamodel)
library(dplyr)      # data-wrangling verbs (mutate, inner_join, ...) used below
library(tidyr)      # pivot_longer, for reshaping the nested/decomposition bands
library(readr)      # read_csv, for the example data files
library(ggplot2)    # the figures below; optional -- skip if you only want the tables
```

Both inputs are files. The active-site table lists one residue per row keyed by `pdb`
and `chain`, so one file can hold many proteins; filter out the chain you are modelling.

```{r inputs}
pdb_file    <- system.file("extdata", "1znb_A.pdb", package = "msamodel")          # <- your PDB
active_file <- system.file("extdata", "znb_active_site.csv", package = "msamodel") # <- your active sites

pdb_site_active <- read_csv(active_file, show_col_types = FALSE) |>
  filter(pdb == "1znb", chain == "A") |>
  pull(pdb_site)

pdb_site_active
```

From those inputs the package reads the structure, builds an elastic network model of it
(`wt`), and runs a **single-point-mutation scan** (`spm`) — mutating every site in turn
and recording how the structure responds. This scan is the raw material the model turns
into a divergence profile. The `set_enm()` and `generate_spm()` arguments below
configure the network model and the scan; leave them at these values to follow along
(the [site analysis](site-analysis.html) vignette covers what they do and how to tune
them). Only the two file paths above are yours to change.

```{r build}
wt_pdb <- bio3d::read.pdb(pdb_file)
wt     <- set_enm(wt_pdb, node = "ca", model = "ming_wall", d_max = 10.5, frustrated = FALSE)
spm    <- generate_spm(wt, n_mutations = 10, model = "lfenm", sigma = 0.3,
                            min_sd = 2, pdb_site_active = pdb_site_active, seed = 1024)
```

The scan takes a minute or two — it is the one slow step. `spm` is what every later
function reads from: for each mutant it records the energy changes and the structural
response it causes, per residue (`dr2mat_site`) and per normal mode (`dr2mat_mode`),
plus the `site_map` from the site index `i` to `pdb_site`.

The last input is your **data**: the observed divergence profile you want to fit — which
residues you measured, and the log-divergence measured at each. Producing it — comparing
homologous structures over an alignment — is outside `msamodel`. You pass it as **two
vectors**, so whatever table it already lives in needs no reshaping. The package ships
one for the worked example:

```{r obs}
observed_site_file <- system.file("extdata", "znb_lrmsd_obs_site.csv", package = "msamodel")
observed_site      <- read_csv(observed_site_file, show_col_types = FALSE)   # <- your observed data
head(observed_site)
```

## A2. Fit `(a1, a2)` to the data

`fit_lrmsd_msa_site()` is the fit. It searches for the `(a1, a2)` that make the model's
predicted profile best match your observed one (by maximum likelihood), and returns the
estimate together with the information needed to put uncertainties on it. It matches on
**shape**, not level: internally it mean-centres both the predicted and the observed
profile before comparing them, so the fit responds only to the *relative* pattern of
divergence along the chain — which is all an observed profile pins down — and the overall
level, which the data does not constrain, drops out. Give it the scan and your two
vectors:

```{r site-fit}
fit_site <- fit_lrmsd_msa_site(spm, observed_site$pdb_site, observed_site$lrmsd_obs)
c(a1 = fit_site$a1, a2 = fit_site$a2)
```

Those two numbers are the fitted stability and activity selection strengths. The fit
object also carries their standard errors, so you know how well-pinned-down each is:

```{r site-se}
c(se_a1 = fit_site$se_a1, se_a2 = fit_site$se_a2)
```

### How well does it fit?

Before trusting a fit, ask how much of the data it actually explains. You do not need
another call: the fit carries a one-row summary in the style of `broom::glance()`,
computed at the optimum.

```{r gof}
fit_site$gof
```

The headline number is `D2`, the fraction of the profile's variance the model captures
(1 = perfect, 0 = no better than predicting the flat average; it can go negative for a
fit worse than that). `AIC` and `BIC` are here for comparing this fit against another
fit of the same data — they are not meaningful in isolation. The remaining columns
(`logLik`, `deviance`, `null_deviance`, `nobs`, `k`, `sigma_hat`) are the raw quantities
`D2`, `AIC`, and `BIC` are built from, exposed in case you want them; for a quick read,
`D2` is the one to look at.

Every band in the rest of Part A is built from `fit_site`.

## A3. Predict the divergence profile, with a band

Now the payoff: the predicted profile. `predict_profiles()` takes the fit and the scan
and returns, on each axis, one row per residue with the profile value and its standard
error. `metric` selects the quantity — `"lrmsd"` for the raw curve or `"nlrmsd"` for the
centred shape. We read the `$site` tibble here.

### The raw curve (`lrmsd`)

```{r site-predict}
predicted_raw_site <- predict_profiles(fit_site, spm, metric = "lrmsd")$site   # lrmsd_msa + lrmsd_msa_se
head(predicted_raw_site)
```

### The centred shape (`nlrmsd`)

The centred prediction returns the same profile with its mean removed, and its own —
narrower — standard error:

```{r site-predict-n}
predicted_site <- predict_profiles(fit_site, spm, metric = "nlrmsd")$site  # nlrmsd_msa + nlrmsd_msa_se
head(predicted_site)
```

### Comparing the fit to your data

To see how well the fitted model matches the measured profile, plot them together. Use
`nlrmsd`, since (as explained above) measured profiles compare on shape, not absolute
level. First centre your observed profile the same way — subtract its mean:

```{r obs-centred}
observed_centred_site <- observed_site |>
  mutate(nlrmsd_obs = lrmsd_obs - mean(lrmsd_obs)) |>
  select(pdb_site, nlrmsd_obs)
```

There is one wrinkle to get right, and it is worth understanding because it will apply
to your own data too. "Centred" only means anything relative to *which residues you
averaged over*. The prediction centres over **all** residues the model covers, because it
knows nothing about your particular dataset. But your measured profile centres over only
the residues *it* observes — and an alignment rarely observes every modelled residue
(here the model spans a few more than the data measures). Two curves centred over two
different residue sets sit at two slightly different zero-levels, so laid on top of each
other they would appear vertically offset for no real reason.

The fix is to put the prediction on the data's footing: re-centre it over exactly the
residues the data observes. Concretely, subtract the prediction's mean *taken over just
the matched residues*. This is one number subtracted from the value, so it slides the
curve (and its band, which we build from `_se`) up or down without changing its
**width** — it only lines the two curves up on a common zero. We build the band from the
standard error at the same time:

```{r site-band-shift}
predicted_band_site <- predicted_site |>
  inner_join(observed_centred_site, by = "pdb_site") |>              # keep only residues the data observes
  mutate(shift = mean(nlrmsd_msa),                       # prediction's mean over those residues
         value = nlrmsd_msa - shift,
         lower = value - z_975 * nlrmsd_msa_se,
         upper = value + z_975 * nlrmsd_msa_se)
```

```{r site-band, eval = has_ggplot}
ggplot(predicted_band_site, aes(pdb_site)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(aes(y = value), colour = "steelblue") +
  geom_point(aes(y = nlrmsd_obs), colour = "grey25", size = 0.7, alpha = 0.7) +
  labs(title = "Site: fitted profile with 95% band vs. observed",
       x = "Residue (PDB numbering)", y = "nlrmsd (shape, mean removed)")
```

The blue band is the model's 95% prediction; the grey points are your data. Where points
sit inside the band, the fit accounts for them.

## A4. Nested models and the decomposition

Two related readings come from one call, `predict_decomposition(fit_site, spm, metric =
"nlrmsd")`. It returns, per axis, both:

- the four **nested-model variants** — the *same* profile under each selection regime:
  **MM**, no selection (mutation only); **MS**, stability alone; **MA**, activity alone;
  **MSA**, the full model — each a centred profile with its standard error;
- the **decomposition** — the full profile split into three additive parts (**mutation**,
  **stability**, **activity**), each with its standard error.

The nested models are four separate what-if profiles you compare; the decomposition
splits the one full profile into contributions you read residue by residue. Both are
reported on the centred (`nlrmsd`) metric — there is no meaningful way to band an overall
level the data cannot see — so `metric = "nlrmsd"` is the reading here:

```{r site-decomp}
decomposition_site <- predict_decomposition(fit_site, spm, metric = "nlrmsd")$site
names(decomposition_site)
```

### The nested-model variants

The nested columns are `nlrmsd_{mm,ms,ma,msa}`, each with an `_se` column further along
(the value columns come first, then the matching `_se` columns). We build a
band from the standard error and plot MM, MS, and MSA on one shape scale (MA omitted to
keep the panel readable):

```{r site-nested-plot, eval = has_ggplot}
model_colours <- c(MM = "#FF8C00", MS = "#0000CD", MSA = "#C41E3A")
# Reshape the value columns and the _se columns to long form separately, then join:
# one row per (residue, model) carrying both the value and its standard error.
nested_value_site <- decomposition_site |>
  select(pdb_site, nlrmsd_mm, nlrmsd_ms, nlrmsd_msa) |>
  pivot_longer(-pdb_site, names_to = "model", names_prefix = "nlrmsd_", values_to = "value")
nested_se_site <- decomposition_site |>
  select(pdb_site, nlrmsd_mm_se, nlrmsd_ms_se, nlrmsd_msa_se) |>
  pivot_longer(-pdb_site, names_to = "model",
               names_pattern = "nlrmsd_(mm|ms|msa)_se", values_to = "se")
nested_long_site <- inner_join(nested_value_site, nested_se_site, by = c("pdb_site", "model")) |>
  mutate(model = factor(toupper(model), levels = c("MM", "MS", "MSA")),
         lower = value - z_975 * se, upper = value + z_975 * se)

ggplot(nested_long_site, aes(pdb_site, colour = model, fill = model)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = pdb_site_active, colour = "firebrick",
             alpha = 0.4, linewidth = 0.3) +     # active-site residues
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), colour = NA, alpha = 0.18) +
  geom_line(aes(y = value), alpha = 0.85) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = model_colours) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = model_colours) +
  labs(title = "Site: nested-model profiles with 95% bands (ML)",
       x = "Residue (PDB numbering)", y = "nlrmsd (shape)", colour = "model", fill = "model")
```

MM's curve varies residue to residue like any profile (mutations perturb different sites
by different amounts). Its band is the **narrowest** of the four: with both selection
strengths off it does not depend on `(a1, a2)`, so the parameter part of its uncertainty
is zero, leaving only the scan-sampling part. Bands widen on the other variants as
selection is switched on and the parameter part enters, and the gap between MM and the
others is the divergence that selection accounts for.

### The decomposition

The decomposition columns are `nphi_{mut,stab,act}` with an `_se` sibling each — `nphi` is
the contribution to the centred profile (the `n` prefix is the same "centred" mark as in
`nlrmsd`). They give each residue's divergence split into how much comes from **mutation**
alone, from **stability** selection, and from **activity** selection:

```{r site-decomposition-plot, eval = has_ggplot}
# Paper component colours: mutation = orange, stability = blue, activity = red.
component_colours <- c(nphi_mut = "#FF8C00", nphi_stab = "#0000CD", nphi_act = "#C41E3A")
decomposition_value_site <- decomposition_site |>
  select(pdb_site, nphi_mut, nphi_stab, nphi_act) |>
  pivot_longer(-pdb_site, names_to = "component", values_to = "value")
decomposition_se_site <- decomposition_site |>
  select(pdb_site, nphi_mut_se, nphi_stab_se, nphi_act_se) |>
  pivot_longer(-pdb_site, names_to = "component",
               names_pattern = "(nphi_(?:mut|stab|act))_se", values_to = "se")
decomposition_long_site <- inner_join(decomposition_value_site, decomposition_se_site, by = c("pdb_site", "component")) |>
  mutate(component = factor(component, levels = names(component_colours)),
         lower = value - z_975 * se, upper = value + z_975 * se)

ggplot(decomposition_long_site, aes(pdb_site, colour = component, fill = component)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = pdb_site_active, colour = "firebrick",
             alpha = 0.4, linewidth = 0.3) +     # active-site residues
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "grey80") +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), colour = NA, alpha = 0.2) +
  geom_line(aes(y = value)) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = component_colours) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = component_colours) +
  labs(title = "Site: divergence decomposition with 95% bands (ML)",
       x = "Residue (PDB numbering)", y = "nphi (contribution, log-divergence units)",
       colour = NULL, fill = NULL)
```

The three parts add up, residue by residue, to the full centred profile `nlrmsd_msa`
(the split is exact). Their bands do **not** add — each is the uncertainty of that one
part. The mutation part (`nphi_mut`) has the narrowest band, for the same reason MM does:
its parameter part is zero (it does not depend on `(a1, a2)`), leaving only scan sampling.

# Part B — Mode axis (read `$mode`)

Everything in Part A read the `$site` tibble — one value per residue. The **mode** axis
runs the identical five-step workflow, reading the `$mode` tibble instead, on the
structure's normal modes: coordinates that describe how the whole protein flexes,
ordered from softest motion upward. Where the site axis answers "which *residues* diverge
most," the mode axis answers "which *collective motions* diverge most" — use it when you
care about the protein's large-scale flexibility rather than individual positions. (What a
normal mode is, and why it is a natural coordinate for divergence, is developed in the
[mode analysis](mode-analysis.html) vignette.)

Each step below is the mode counterpart of the same-numbered site step, so the
*reasoning* from Part A carries over unchanged — here we read `$mode` and point out only
what genuinely differs on this axis.

## B1. Setup (mode)

You work from the same `spm` object built in A1; the mode reading uses its per-mode
response matrix `dr2mat_mode`. Your data on this axis is a mode-indexed profile, columns
`mode` and `lrmsd_obs`. The example profile shipped for this axis is **synthetic** (the
`_syn` in the file name): no empirical per-mode profile exists, so it was generated from
the site fit plus seeded noise purely to exercise the machinery. Treat the numbers below
as a demonstration of the method, not a result about this protein.

```{r mode-data}
observed_mode_file <- system.file("extdata", "znb_lrmsd_obs_mode_syn.csv", package = "msamodel")
observed_mode      <- read_csv(observed_mode_file, show_col_types = FALSE)  # <- your observed mode profile
head(observed_mode)
```

## B2. Fit (mode)

Same fit, mode data:

```{r mode-fit}
fit_mode <- fit_lrmsd_msa_mode(spm, observed_mode$mode, observed_mode$lrmsd_obs)
c(a1 = fit_mode$a1, a2 = fit_mode$a2, se_a1 = fit_mode$se_a1, se_a2 = fit_mode$se_a2)
```

...and, as on the site axis, the fit carries its own goodness of fit:

```{r mode-gof}
fit_mode$gof
```

## B3. Predict the profile (mode)

The same profile prediction, read from `$mode`:

```{r mode-predict}
predicted_mode <- predict_profiles(fit_mode, spm, metric = "nlrmsd")$mode   # nlrmsd_msa + its _se
head(predicted_mode)
```

Two differences from the site axis, both because modes are not residues. There is no
`pdb_site` column — modes are indexed by `mode`, so the observed overlay joins on `mode`. And
here **every mode is observed** (the data covers all of the model's modes), so the
re-levelling wrinkle from A3 does not arise: the prediction and the data are already
centred over the same set, and we join directly with no shift.

```{r mode-band-obs}
observed_centred_mode <- observed_mode |>
  mutate(nlrmsd_obs = lrmsd_obs - mean(lrmsd_obs)) |>
  select(mode, nlrmsd_obs)

predicted_band_mode <- predicted_mode |>
  inner_join(observed_centred_mode, by = "mode") |>
  mutate(lower = nlrmsd_msa - z_975 * nlrmsd_msa_se,
         upper = nlrmsd_msa + z_975 * nlrmsd_msa_se)
```

```{r mode-band, eval = has_ggplot}
ggplot(predicted_band_mode, aes(mode)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), fill = "seagreen", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(aes(y = nlrmsd_msa), colour = "seagreen") +
  geom_point(aes(y = nlrmsd_obs), colour = "grey25", size = 0.5, alpha = 0.5) +
  labs(title = "Mode: fitted profile with 95% band vs. observed",
       x = "Mode index n", y = "nlrmsd (shape, mean removed)")
```

## B4. Nested models and the decomposition (mode)

The mode reading of `predict_decomposition()`, as in A4:

```{r mode-decomp}
decomposition_mode <- predict_decomposition(fit_mode, spm, metric = "nlrmsd")$mode
```

```{r mode-nested-plot, eval = has_ggplot}
model_colours <- c(MM = "#FF8C00", MS = "#0000CD", MSA = "#C41E3A")
nested_value_mode <- decomposition_mode |>
  select(mode, nlrmsd_mm, nlrmsd_ms, nlrmsd_msa) |>
  pivot_longer(-mode, names_to = "model", names_prefix = "nlrmsd_", values_to = "value")
nested_se_mode <- decomposition_mode |>
  select(mode, nlrmsd_mm_se, nlrmsd_ms_se, nlrmsd_msa_se) |>
  pivot_longer(-mode, names_to = "model",
               names_pattern = "nlrmsd_(mm|ms|msa)_se", values_to = "se")
nested_long_mode <- inner_join(nested_value_mode, nested_se_mode, by = c("mode", "model")) |>
  mutate(model = factor(toupper(model), levels = c("MM", "MS", "MSA")),
         lower = value - z_975 * se, upper = value + z_975 * se)

ggplot(nested_long_mode, aes(mode, colour = model, fill = model)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), colour = NA, alpha = 0.18) +
  geom_line(aes(y = value), alpha = 0.85) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = model_colours) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = model_colours) +
  labs(title = "Mode: nested-model profiles with 95% bands (ML)",
       x = "Mode index n", y = "nlrmsd (shape)", colour = "model", fill = "model")
```

As on the site axis, MM has the narrowest band — its parameter part is zero (it does not
depend on `(a1, a2)`), leaving only the scan-sampling part.

```{r mode-decomposition-plot, eval = has_ggplot}
decomposition_value_mode <- decomposition_mode |>
  select(mode, nphi_mut, nphi_stab, nphi_act) |>
  pivot_longer(-mode, names_to = "component", values_to = "value")
decomposition_se_mode <- decomposition_mode |>
  select(mode, nphi_mut_se, nphi_stab_se, nphi_act_se) |>
  pivot_longer(-mode, names_to = "component",
               names_pattern = "(nphi_(?:mut|stab|act))_se", values_to = "se")
decomposition_long_mode <- inner_join(decomposition_value_mode, decomposition_se_mode, by = c("mode", "component")) |>
  mutate(component = factor(component, levels = names(component_colours)),
         lower = value - z_975 * se, upper = value + z_975 * se)

ggplot(decomposition_long_mode, aes(mode, colour = component, fill = component)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "grey80") +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), colour = NA, alpha = 0.2) +
  geom_line(aes(y = value)) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = component_colours) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = component_colours) +
  labs(title = "Mode: divergence decomposition with 95% bands (ML)",
       x = "Mode index n", y = "nphi (contribution, log-divergence units)",
       colour = NULL, fill = NULL)
```

As on the site axis, the three parts sum to `nlrmsd_msa`, and the mutation part
(`nphi_mut`) has the narrowest band — its parameter part is zero, leaving only scan sampling.

